  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mbeleni Foundation LTD</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mbeleni Foundation seeks support to address causes of GBV by empowering Rights Holders who for this project include Adolescent Girls and Young Women (AGYW) with knowledge and skills that will support them to be self-reliant and advocate for their involvement in decisions that affect their wellbeing. GBV is not only rampant among refugees but also a serious issue among nationals. The situationfor refugees is worse and that is why Mbeleni Foundation seeks to intervene by empowering Refugee AGYW and their host communities with knowledge and skills to prevent and respond to the impact of GBV. Project main objective: To strengthen the capacity of 100Adolescent Girls and Young Women to prevent and respond to the impact of Gender Based Violence in Kyaka II Refugee settlement. Project specific Objectives.
Objective 1: To increase knowledge of rights holders on causes, effects and preventive measures of GBV among AGYWand their need to be involved in decisions that affect their well-being.
Objective 2: To mobilize, train and support Adolescent Girls and Young Women in alternative livelihood
activities.
Objective 3: To promote community-based responses and engagement with key stakeholders in addressing negative socio-economic and cultural practices that perpetuate gender inequalities. By the end of this project, Mbeleni Foundation will reach 100 AGYW as the direct Rights holders; 500 will be indirect Rights holders.
The project will kick off with an inception meeting targeting key stakeholders in the district and the settlement followed by an appraisal to get a moredefined understanding of the situation in the settlement. The two initial activities will inform involved rightsholders on the GBV situation in the area hence a buy-in for the project; and together work out preventive measures.
An Alternative Livelihoods appraisal to list existing Income Generating Activities(IGAs)  will  be conducted. Participants will then rank, select and be supported to uptake viable IGAs. Success stories will be documented and shared during linking and learning sessions.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MBELENI FOUNDATION LIMITED</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MBELENI FOUNDATION LTD a women founded and led organisation that founded in 2006 and legally registered with the NGO Bureau in 2011on recognition of the fact that while there are a number of efforts by government, humanitarian agencies and many other diverse stakeholders to address Refugee&#x2019;s plight in Uganda, the scope is still small and calls for strengthening. Refugees&#x2019; Rights are not fully realized; Refugee women, youth and children&#x2019;s psycho-social, health and educational needs are not met to the fullest, and their orientation to relevant governing laws and policies need to be popularized. Compelled by this, Mbeleni was motivated to join the forceto contribute to bridging the gaps and particularly focus her activities on community socio-economic justice and development. Mbeleni contributes to the realization of community wellbeing with focus on children, youth and women within refugee camps and settlements, the host communities and other populations affected by conflicts, disease, poverty and natural disasters.
From 2018, to-date the Board of Director of Mbeleni Foundation spearheaded by a female constituted a nuclear staff to manage the day today business of the organisation, allocated the organisation an office space, and constructed and approvedfunctional structures and policies for runningthe organisation.
Being a women Led organisation; all key positions (Executive Director, and Board chairperson) must be occupied bywomen. There is a gender policy in place that strategically informs the undertaking of these decisions.

Mbeleni has long transformed and expanded its mandate to other vulnerable and less privileged populations who include, but not limited to refugees, asylum seekers, children, youths, women, girls, Internally Displaced Persons(IDPs) and People with Disabilities (PWDs). The Foundation
also works with the refugee host communities and other populations affected by conflicts, disease, poverty and natural disasters in all heractivities. Mbeleni contributes to the realization ofcommunity wellbeing with focus on children, youth and women within refugee settlements and the host communities.
In the last three years we acquired office space for our head office, we instituted a nuclear staff, put up policies and structures that areoperational, opened a field office with staff in Kyaka II refugee settlement, acquired legal partnership with OPM by signing an MOU, implemented Spicy project, and women leadership project with urban refugee women.
In 2022, we also carried out a number ofsensitization campaigns of gender issues during the International Women&#x2019;s Day week and the 16 Daysof Activism Against GBV by holding 2 Radio talk shows and 2 dialogues for women and Youth awake from COVID 19 projects.
We also linked and referred a group of 10 youth and women to access affordable financial services without providing security to boost up their businesses.</narrative>
